CORE DESIGN DECISIONS (already settled — please keep to these)
1. An "agent" = model + tools + a loop. Claude decides what to do; my Python code
   actually executes it. The basic loop: send messages -> if Claude requests a
   tool, run it and feed the result back -> repeat until Claude gives a final answer.
2. NEVER dump table rows into the model. The warehouse/DB reads every row and
   computes summaries; the model only ever sees small aggregate results plus a
   tiny sample of rows (~20). This keeps cost flat regardless of table size.
3. Data-quality checks are aggregate SQL queries: COUNT(*) vs COUNT(col) for null
   rate, COUNT(DISTINCT col) for cardinality, MIN/MAX for ranges, GROUP BY ...
   HAVING COUNT(*)>1 for duplicates, WHERE <bad pattern> for malformed values
   (return the count + a few example bad rows).
4. The single main tool is `run_sql(query)`. Start against a small LOCAL SQLite
   table so I don't touch real infrastructure or spend warehouse credits while
   learning. Move to a real warehouse (Snowflake/Databricks) only later.

